 -=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-
                Common questions for first-time users of PCBMail:
 -=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-

 Q:  How do I make a PCBMail group in Windows?

 A:  1. Go to the file pull down menu item and select New Group...
                                                      ~~~~~~~~~~~~
     2. Enter in the group name and description into the text fields.

     3. Select New Item... from the file pull down menu.
               ~~~~~~~~~~~
     4. Enter PCBMail in the description field.
              ~~~~~~~
     5. Enter the path and file name of your PCBMAIL.EXE file.

     6. Enter the working directory for PCBMail to use.
        NOTE:  PCBMail needs this directory to store configuration
               information required to run PCBMail.

     Also to execute PCBMail login configuration only, you may execute
     PCBMAIL.EXE with a -CONFIG on the command line prompt.

     For example:  PCBMAIL.EXE -CONFIG

     You may also override the login name and password from the command
     line. The parameters for this is -USER[YOUR NAME] and -PASS[YOUR
     PASSWORD] Thus you can set PCBMail up to run from only one PCB.CFG
     file.

 Q:  I keep getting a dialogue box stating that PCBMail cannot find the
     PCBOARD.DAT filename.

 A:  Most likely it is a problem with your PCBMail login configuration,
     which is not pointing to the correct location of your PCBOARD.DAT
     file. Make sure that in the field that asks you for the location of
     your PCBOARD.DAT filename that you enter the entire name and location.

     For example:  C:\PCB\PCBOARD.DAT


 Q:  I want to run PCBMail with one executable on a server.

 A:  You must have the read only attribute set to read only.  See the help
     documentation for attrib in your DOS documentation. To set the read
     only attribute for PCBMAIL.EXE use [attrib +R pcbmail.exe] This lets
     you share copies of PCBMail over the network.


 Q:  I am running PCBMail from one copy of the PCBOARD.DAT file how do I
     set up PCBMail to work with this?

 A:  Your node number is located in the PCBOARD.DAT filename.  PCBMail has
     a configuration option which overloads this node number with one that
     you specify.  This is located in the login configuration dialogue box,
     which also lets you specify which node number you wish to use when
     logging into PCBMail.


 Q:  When I start PCBMail it will promptly exit without me doing anything?
     PCBMail will work when I am not running over my Novell network on my
     own hard drive.

 A:  This occurs when running NOVELL. This is potentially a problem with
     your BWCC.DLL file being too old. PCBMail does warn you of an outdated
     BWCC.DLL file. What you need to do is find all the occurrences of the
     BWCC.DLL and replace it with the one included with PCBMail.  A problem
     that some systems have running Novell is that Novell will scan
     backwards on your disk for a file. One way of forcing Novell to not do
     this is to place an option on your command line when loading PCBMail.
     An example of this is;

         SMODE 2 c:\pcbmail\pcbmail.exe

     What is SMODE?
         SMODE is a command line utility when specifies the searching mode
         in file retrieval.  SMODE causes problems because NOVELL may
         search in other directories other then the one that is specified
         of the current program.  The '2' in the command line tells NOVELL
         to not search beyond the working directory of the file.

     References to SMODE:
         Novell Netware Version 3.11 Utilities Reference, pp. 383 - 390
         Novell Netware Version 2.2  Using the Network, pp. 431 - 433


 Q:  I want to run PCBMail from one PCB.CFG file how do I do it? I also
     don't want to share my user name and password with other users using
     PCBMail.

 A:  You can override the login name and password from the command line.
     The parameters for this are -USER[YOUR NAME] and -PASS[YOUR PASSWORD]
     Thus you can set PCBMail up to run from only one PCB.CFG file.

